        "The Single European Act, signed in 1986 and in force from 1987, was the first major revision of the Treaties of Rome. Its central aim was to complete the internal market, meaning the free movement of goods, services, people and capital, by the end of 1992. A Commission White Paper published in 1985 had identified around 300 legislative measures needed to remove physical, technical and fiscal barriers between the Member States.\n\nTo make this programme possible, the Act extended the use of qualified majority voting in the Council to most single-market legislation, which until then had often required unanimity; tax matters, however, continued to require unanimity. It also strengthened the role of the European Parliament through a new 'cooperation procedure', which gave the Parliament a second reading and made it harder for the Council to ignore its views, and it gave formal recognition to European Political Cooperation in foreign policy.\n\nNot every barrier disappeared by the deadline. Many measures had been adopted by the end of 1992, but some were transposed late into national law, and in several areas, such as services and public procurement, a fully integrated market remained incomplete for years. Even so, the programme is widely credited with reviving European integration after a period of stagnation.",
